    "md5": "b0801320a7485877e0c7dc7643c6e9e1",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1887,
    "chunk_text": "According to EBSCO (a research organisation), an estimated 40,000 to 60,000 people were killed or went missing during the second JVP insurrection, which peaked fiercely in 1989. An exact tally however is very difficult to\u00a0 get; but human rights organisations generally agree on these figures.\n\nIt seems the wheel has turned full circle. The JVP/NPP government\u00a0 today apparently\u00a0 understands India has no ulterior designs on little Lanka. Big brother India is really our best friend. How sad\u2026 so many thousands of young men and woman died in vain,\u00a0 and so much material damage was caused to our country.\n\nSince coming into power,\u00a0 the present government has also made a \u2018few\u2019 mistakes. Before being elected into power at the presidential and parliamentary elections, the NPP/JVP coalition promised to bring down the cost of living. Unfortunately,\u00a0 they have not been able to live up to expectations.\n\nAccording to the Department of Census and Statistics July 2026 report, between 20% to 25% of the population remains affected by poverty. Since January 2026 the national minimum wage stands at Rs. 30,000/- per month. But a family of four (father mother and two children) needs\u00a0 a minimum over Rs. 100,000/- to have two square meals a day!\n\nEven if both parents worked, their take home wage will not be able to cover the cost of meals alone. Not surprisingly,\u00a0 More than 20% (nearly one in five) of schoolchildren in Sri Lanka suffer from malnutrition and there is no indication wages will increase. In fact wages of employees have not been paid by a number of employers.\n\nHowever the parliamentary Opposition has not been able to capitalise on suffering of the people. Even today,\u00a0 while malnutrition stalks the land and wages are not keeping pace with the rising cost of living, parties in opposition are taking up the issue of the retirement age of judges in the country!"
  },
