  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-12-dailymirrorlk-d44af714",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-d44af714",
    "date_str": "2026-09-12",
    "description": "How do American  sanctions on  Iran affect  Sri Lanka?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/How-do-American-sanctions-on-Iran-affect-Sri-Lanka/172-350119",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789183445.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-12-dailymirrorlk-d44af714-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "d78f6505dad071bb98bf08abc2b96b0d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1601,
    "chunk_text": "How do American  sanctions on  Iran affect  Sri Lanka?\n\nIranian leaders know that control of the Strait of Hormuz is a strategic weapon\n\nYet the economic impact of the crisis is substantial, and the estimated cost varies from US$ 1.3 trillion to 3.5 trillion, depending on how long the blockade continues. The World Bank has lowered the global economic growth forecast to 2.5%\n\nThe Trump administration\u2019s handling of the conflict with Iran has an apt local simile: That is how Sri Lanka plays cricket these days. Both reveal neither strategy nor conviction, and run on whims. America\u2019s masterful mismanagement of Iran has now turned a containable threat into a global contingency.\n\nPresident Donald Trump has blown hot and cold against the Islamic Republic of Iran, bombed its nuclear sites while the regime negotiators were at the table, and now the bruised Ayatollahs left with a large stockpile of enriched uranium are ever more determined to pursue their nuclear dream.\n\nSince\u00a0 Trump is more amenable to rogue leaders with nuclear weapons, such as North Korea\u2019s Kim dynasty, the Ayatollahs would be right to believe a nuclear bomb would not only be a deterrent; it might also open doors to Donald Trump\u2019s good graces.\n\nThe American air strikes in February killed Supreme Leader Ayatollah Khamenei, only to be succeeded by his hardliner son, and forced the regime firmly into the hands of hardliners. The war also vanquished any prospect of popular opposition to the regime any time soon. Instead of defanging the regime, America\u2019s war of choice has tightened its grip on the population."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-09-12-dailymirrorlk-d44af714",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-d44af714",
    "date_str": "2026-09-12",
    "description": "How do American  sanctions on  Iran affect  Sri Lanka?",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/opinion/How-do-American-sanctions-on-Iran-affect-Sri-Lanka/172-350119",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1789183445.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-09-12-dailymirrorlk-d44af714-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6ac223a98b912bf88be9f4170f23729f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2001,
    "chunk_text": "The American air strikes in February killed Supreme Leader Ayatollah Khamenei, only to be succeeded by his hardliner son, and forced the regime firmly into the hands of hardliners. The war also vanquished any prospect of popular opposition to the regime any time soon. Instead of defanging the regime, America\u2019s war of choice has tightened its grip on the population.\n\nThe ceasefire expired in August, and the Ayatollahs, instead of biting the carrot, realised that control of the Strait of Hormuz is a strategic weapon as consequential as nukes. The Iranian demand for sovereignty over the Strait of Hormuz and the right to levy tolls on\u00a0 ships that sail through the strait,\u00a0 through which 30 per cent of the world\u2019s seaborne crude oil passes daily,\u00a0 would shatter the long-existing consensus of maritime commons. It could also set a precedent for the other states to follow \u2014 though such a misadventure would entail a disproportionate reputational and economic cost.\n\nLast month, the Trump administration announced \u2018Operation Economic Outcast\u2019 to \u2018sever every economic lifeline\u2019 that sustains the regime in Tehran. The new sanctions were aimed at crypto, technology, gold, aviation and shipping, and Washington threatened to remove any third-party violator from the US Dollar system.\n\nThe efficacy of these last resort sanctions is yet to be seen. President Trump has called the new sanctions \u2018Economic D-Day One \u2019, invoking the Normandy landing of World War II.\u00a0 However, the success or failure of the latest strategy would be decided far from Washington or Tehran \u2014 and obviously not in Normandy \u2014 but in Beijing, which buys 80 per cent of Iranian oil at a premium discount. China has not heeded American calls, nor does the Trump administration, which habitually bullies its Western allies,\u00a0 seem to dare to antagonise the Middle Kingdom. Mr Trump recently announced tariffs on Canadian aircraft maker Bombardier, and accused Canada of treating America as a piggy bank."
  },
  {
