    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "2bdedb50f6eec3a9db15e31d705c5767",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1979,
    "chunk_text": "Grenades become weapon of fear: Available for as little as Rs. 10,000 \u2013 20,000\n\nColombo, Sep. 12 (Daily Mirror) - Hand grenades are increasingly being used by organised criminal groups to threaten rivals and settle disputes, raising concerns among security authorities over the availability of old military explosives and the danger they pose to the public.\n\nThe Daily Mirror learns that the explosives can reportedly be obtained on Sri Lanka\u2019s black market for as little as Rs. 10,000 to Rs. 20,000, according to security sources.\n\n\u201cThey are available through various sources, including areas associated with former military personnel, where hand grenades may remain buried. If someone seeks to purchase one, it can be obtained for around Rs. 10,000 to Rs. 20,000, even without the knowledge of the original owner,\u201d the sources revealed.\n\nThe availability of such explosives has come to attention following yesterday's incident in Kawdana, Dehiwala, which claimed the lives of two children and seriously injured their father.\n\nPolice investigations found that the family was not believed to have been the intended target. The explosive was allegedly meant for a neighbouring residence linked to an underworld dispute but was thrown at the wrong house.\n\nA senior police officer said criminal groups often use such devices primarily as a means of intimidation rather than as a direct attempt to kill.\n\n\u201cThey throw a bomb to frighten someone and leave. It is mainly used to threaten members of rival groups or people involved in disputes over drugs and other criminal activities,\u201d the officer said.\n\nSecurity sources said firearms have become harder to obtain amid ongoing police operations against organised crime, leading some groups to turn to locally made weapons and explosives.\n\nOf particular concern are old military hand grenades believed to have remained in illegal stockpiles. Security officials said some can remain dangerous for years if properly preserved."
  },
