    "md5": "cacfa5213a4c3a65a1bd1a1d7b17a538",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1858,
    "chunk_text": "Security sources said firearms have become harder to obtain amid ongoing police operations against organised crime, leading some groups to turn to locally made weapons and explosives.\n\nOf particular concern are old military hand grenades believed to have remained in illegal stockpiles. Security officials said some can remain dangerous for years if properly preserved.\n\n\u201cThere are no weapons; nowadays, even for shootings, they mostly use improvised firearms or local single-barrel guns. Those are locally made and brought out. Weapons are very rare for them,\u201d the officer said.\n\nThe recent incidents have raised concerns that explosives are becoming an increasingly common means of intimidation in disputes involving organised crime.\n\nOn September 9, two men travelling on a motorcycle allegedly threw a hand grenade into the premises of a house in Kalubowila, Dehiwala, before fleeing. The blast damaged windows and glass panes at the front of the residence, but no injuries were reported. Police said the property belonged to an individual believed to be a close associate of a major drug trafficker. Only a 56-year-old woman was inside at the time.\n\nEarlier, on August 23, a house on Lesley Ranagala Mawatha in Borella was targeted. The grenade failed to explode, and no injuries were reported. Police said the residence was allegedly linked to an associate of organised-crime figure \u201cWanathe Chathu,\u201d who was recently arrested in Dubai.\n\nAnother incident was reported on August 5 on Sri Gnanendra Mawatha in Ratmalana, where two grenades were allegedly thrown at a property. One exploded, but no injuries were reported. Police said the premises were located close to the residence of the mother of alleged drug trafficker \u201cRussell Smith.\u201d\n\nPolice suspect the incidents are linked to an ongoing conflict between rival organised-crime groups."
