    "md5": "67754c044cc9c02d257834be6de72129",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1964,
    "chunk_text": "Dehiwala \u2018Made in China\u2019 footwear racket exposed\n\nColombo, Sep. 13 (Daily Mirror) - The Consumer Affairs Authority (CAA) has raided a clandestine footwear manufacturing facility in Kawdana, Dehiwala, where footwear allegedly assembled locally was being labelled as \u201cMade in China\u201d and supplied to reputed retail chains.\n\nThe raid was carried out on September 11 following a lengthy investigation into invoices and the supply chain of footwear purchased from a well-known clothing and fashion retail chain.\n\nAccording to the CAA, investigations revealed that soles and other components of the footwear had been imported separately as raw materials, allegedly to avoid import duties applicable to finished footwear.\n\nThe imported components were subsequently assembled and glued together at the facility in Kawdana to produce complete pairs of shoes.\n\nInvestigators further found that the footwear assembled locally was packaged and labelled as \u201cMade in China\u201d before being distributed to well-known retail outlets. The products allegedly did not carry the name and address of the actual manufacturer.\n\nThe CAA said such practices could mislead consumers about the actual country of manufacture and make it difficult to identify the responsible manufacturer in the event of defects or other issues with the footwear.\n\nThe Authority also warned that such practices could create unfair competition for local manufacturers and traders who comply with tax and other legal requirements.\n\nDuring the raid, CAA officers seized nearly 1,000 pairs of footwear along with raw materials used for their assembly.\n\nFurther investigations are underway, and legal action will be taken against those responsible upon completion of the investigations, the CAA said.\n\nThe Authority said it would continue conducting follow-up raids based on invoices and supply-chain information to curb illegal trade practices, protect consumers and ensure fair competition in the market."
