    "md5": "b87fdc0008e6e37b9b3151f54cc1b3b7",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1958,
    "chunk_text": "Drought tests humanity as wildlife struggles for survival\n\nColombo, Sep. 13 (Daily Mirror) - As dry conditions intensify across the country amid the effects of El Ni\u00f1o, the Ministry of Environment has warned of the growing challenges faced by both people and wildlife, highlighted that compassion and responsible human behaviour are essential to protect animals during the difficult period.\n\nThe Ministry said the arid conditions were a shared natural challenge affecting both humans and wildlife, prompting the Department of Wildlife Conservation (DWC), together with institutions affiliated with the Ministry, to implement emergency measures to support animals affected by the drought.\n\n\u201cThirst and hunger are burdens shared by both humans and beasts. During these harsh dry spells, ensuring that wildlife is kept out of harm\u2019s way is a responsibility shared by all,\u201d the Ministry said.\n\nAccording to the Ministry, water is being transported by bowsers to reservoirs inside national parks and protected areas, while artificial waterholes are being maintained and fodder provided to wildlife facing food shortages.\n\nThe Ministry also said that, with the support of the tri-forces, continuous efforts were being made to rescue stranded wildlife and provide urgent veterinary care to wild elephants and other animals injured as a result of human activities.\n\nIt stressed that wildlife was facing additional dangers from gunshots, explosive devices such as \u2018hakka patas\u2019, illegal electric fences and poisoning.\n\n\u201cWhile the drought is a natural challenge, the suffering caused by human cruelty is preventable. Shooting animals, setting up illegal electric fences or laying poison can turn an already difficult situation into a tragedy,\u201d the Ministry said.\n\nThe Ministry urged the public, particularly communities living close to wildlife habitats, to refrain from harming animals and instead allow them to move safely in search of water and food."
